### What happened?

`submission.wait()` returns `done` when the model stops with `length` and its only content is `codemode({})`. The submission's `answer` points to that tool call. The transcript has no `pi.tool-result` and no final text. A caller that checks the submission status sees a completed input.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Save the script below as `repro.mjs` in an empty directory and install the listed packages. It uses Pi's `fauxProvider`, `MemoryStorage` and built-in `codemode` tool.
2. Run `node repro.mjs`. The harness makes one model request and returns the unexecuted tool call as the answer.
3. Run `node repro.mjs toolUse`. With the same `{}` arguments, the harness records a tool error, makes a second model request and returns `follow-up answer`.

<details><summary>Install command and standalone script</summary>

```sh
npm init -y
npm install --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-durable@1.6.0 @earendil-works/pi-ai@1.6.0 @earendil-works/pi-codemode@1.6.0 @earendil-works/chord@1.6.0
```

```js
import { BACKGROUND_CONTEXT as ctx } from "@earendil-works/chord/context";
import { createModels, fauxAssistantMessage, fauxProvider, fauxToolCall } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai";
import { CodemodeSandbox } from "@earendil-works/pi-codemode";
import { AssistantEntry, createRegistry, Harness, MemoryStorage, ToolResultEntry } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable";
import { createCodemodeExtension } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable/codemode";

const [stopReason = "length", args = "{}"] = process.argv.slice(2);
const faux = fauxProvider();
faux.setResponses([
  fauxAssistantMessage([fauxToolCall("codemode", JSON.parse(args), { id: "call-1" })], { stopReason }),
  fauxAssistantMessage("follow-up answer"),
]);
const models = createModels();
models.setProvider(faux.provider);
const registry = createRegistry();
registry.install(createCodemodeExtension({ sandbox: new CodemodeSandbox(), models: false }));

const harness = await Harness.open(new MemoryStorage(), { models, registry }, ctx);
try {
  const root = await harness.root(ctx, { agent: { model: { provider: "faux", modelId: "faux-1" } } });
  harness.resume();
  const submission = await root.submit({ type: "input", content: "Run codemode, then reply." }, ctx);
  const settled = await submission.wait(ctx);
  const { items } = await root.entries({}, 100, undefined, ctx);
  const toolResults = items.filter((entry) => ToolResultEntry.is(entry)).map((entry) => entry.model[0]);
  const answer = settled.answer && await root.commit((tx) => tx.entry(AssistantEntry, settled.answer), ctx);
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    status: settled.status,
    modelCalls: faux.state.callCount,
    answerStopReason: answer?.model[0].stopReason,
    answerContent: answer?.model[0].content,
    toolResults: toolResults.map(({ toolCallId, isError }) => ({ toolCallId, isError })),
  }, null, 2));
} finally {
  await harness.close(ctx);
}
```

</details>

### Expected behavior

Keep the truncated call unexecuted. Give callers a way to distinguish an unanswered input at settlement, or record a truncation error for the tool call so the model can respond.

### Version

`@earendil-works/pi-durable@1.6.0`, Node.js `24.22.1`, macOS arm64. The reproduction ran with network access disabled.

### What happened?

On Pi 1.6.0, `/new` briefly shows the built-in footer before the custom footer returns. I reproduced it in regular and fullscreen modes with only the extension below. The same behavior was [reported in a comment on the related header issue #11025](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/issues/11025#issuecomment-6211067389).

### Steps to reproduce

Save as `footer-repro.mjs`:

```js
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("session_start", ({ reason }, ctx) => {
    ctx.ui.setFooter(() => ({
      invalidate() {},
      render: () => [`CUSTOM FOOTER (${reason})`, ""],
    }));
  });
}
```

Run `pi --offline -ne --no-session -e ./footer-repro.mjs`, then type `/new`.

### Expected behavior

Switch directly to the new custom footer. I have a tested patch that keeps the last rendered rows visible without keeping the old extension alive. Happy to prepare a PR.

### Version

1.6.0

### Summary

`iterateSseMessages` in `packages/ai/src/api/anthropic-messages.ts` treats a CRLF line terminator as two line breaks when the `\r` and the `\n` arrive in different chunks. The pending event is then flushed with no `data:` field, `parseJsonWithRepair("")` throws, and the assistant message fails with:

```
Could not parse Anthropic SSE event content_block_delta: Unexpected end of JSON input; data=; raw=event: content_block_delta
```

Per the WHATWG SSE spec, CRLF is a single line terminator regardless of how the bytes are chunked, so this is a parser bug rather than a server problem. It is not a rare path in practice: the proxy we use always sends CRLF, and this aborted ~2% of my assistant turns (7 of 506).

### What happens

`consumeLine` only collapses `\r\n` when both bytes are already in the buffer:

```ts
function consumeLine(text) {
    const lineBreakIndex = nextLineBreakIndex(text);
    if (lineBreakIndex === -1) return null;
    let nextIndex = lineBreakIndex + 1;
    if (text[lineBreakIndex] === "\r" && text[nextIndex] === "\n") {
        nextIndex += 1;   // only works when both bytes are in `text`
    }
    return { line: text.slice(0, lineBreakIndex), rest: text.slice(nextIndex) };
}
```

If a chunk ends with `...event: content_block_delta\r`:

1. `\r` is consumed as the line break and the buffer is left empty.
2. The next chunk starts with `\n`, which is now an empty line → `flushSseEvent` emits `{ event: "content_block_delta", data: [], raw: ["event: content_block_delta"] }`.
3. `iterateAnthropicEvents` calls `parseJsonWithRepair("")` → `Unexpected end of JSON input`, wrapped into the message above.

`consumeLine` keeps no state for "the previous chunk ended with `\r`", so the information needed to do the right thing is already gone when the next chunk arrives.

### Reproduction (offline, no network)

`min_repro.mjs` replays a handful of frames with an injected `fetch`, cutting the body at exactly `event: content_block_delta\r` | `\ndata: ...`:

```
single read                  -> OK
split between \r and \n      -> ERROR: Could not parse Anthropic SSE event content_block_delta: Unexpected end of JSON input; data=; raw=event: content_block_delta
```

Using the real `stream()` with an injected `fetch` and a captured CRLF SSE body (`raw_p6.sse`, 4558 bytes, 21 frames), splitting the body at every byte offset:

- **21 of 4558 offsets fail, exactly one per frame**, all of them at the `\r` | `\n` boundary, all producing the error text above verbatim.
- The same capture rewritten with LF endings: **0 of 4495 offsets fail**.
- For comparison, truncating the stream at arbitrary offsets yields `data=<half JSON>` 82% of the time and `stream ended before message_stop` 13% of the time, so the "empty data" signature is not truncation.

### Why it aborts the turn

The error message does not match `RETRYABLE_PROVIDER_ERROR_PATTERN` (`packages/ai/src/utils/retry.ts`), so `isRetryableAssistantError` returns false and the run stops instead of retrying. Independently of the parser fix, it may be worth treating SSE parse failures as retryable, since they are recoverable by nature.

### Suggested fix

Carry the state across chunks — remember that the previous chunk ended with `\r` and drop a leading `\n` of the next chunk, or hold back a trailing `\r` until more data arrives:

```ts
function consumeLine(text, atEof = false) {
    const lineBreakIndex = nextLineBreakIndex(text);
    if (lineBreakIndex === -1) return null;
    // A trailing "\r" may be the first half of a CRLF split across chunks.
    if (!atEof && text[lineBreakIndex] === "\r" && lineBreakIndex === text.length - 1) return null;
    let nextIndex = lineBreakIndex + 1;
    if (text[lineBreakIndex] === "\r" && text[nextIndex] === "\n") nextIndex += 1;
    return { line: text.slice(0, lineBreakIndex), rest: text.slice(nextIndex) };
}
```

…with `atEof = true` for the drain loops after `done`. I have been running this locally: the all-offset scan goes from 21 failures to 0 on both `dist/api/anthropic-messages.js` and the CLI bundle, with no other change in output.

### Versions

- `@earendil-works/pi-ai` 0.99.2 and 1.0.0 — `dist/api/anthropic-messages.js` is byte-identical between them, so the bug is present in the latest release.
- Provider: Anthropic Messages API through an internal proxy (`Server: volcalb`), `content-type: text/event-stream`, `transfer-encoding: chunked`, CRLF line endings; frames were aligned to HTTP chunk boundaries in every capture I took.
- Node 24.21.0, Linux x86_64.

### Notes

I could not capture a live `\r` | `\n` split — 9 small requests all happened to split on frame boundaries. The trigger needs long streams with dense deltas (tool-call `input_json_delta`), which is exactly where all 7 observed failures happened (42–100 s into the stream, mid tool-call arguments). The all-offset scan shows the failure is deterministic once a split lands there, so a live capture is not needed to confirm the mechanism; I can add one if it would help.

## Observed
Two pi processes that open the same `auth.json` through different paths (e.g. one through a symlink) can enter the locked credential-modification section at the same time. Concurrent OAuth refreshes can then race on the same refresh token and the same underlying file.

Seen on @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent 1.3.0 (Linux); the code is unchanged in v1.4.0 and on main (2c8d1feb):
- https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/v1.4.0/packages/coding-agent/src/core/auth-storage.ts#L81-L90
- https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/v1.4.0/packages/coding-agent/src/core/auth-storage.ts#L117-L137

Both sync and async acquisition call proper-lockfile with `realpath: false`, and `normalizePath` expands path syntax but doesn't resolve symlinks. So each alias gets its own lock.

How we hit it: benchmark harnesses that give each run its own agent dir with `auth.json` symlinked to the main one, so all runs share one login.

## Minimal reproduction (no credentials, no network)
Creates a temporary `{}` auth.json and one symlink to it. The parent holds the async lock, first via the canonical path and then via the alias, while a child process tries to lock the canonical path with a 500 ms abort deadline.

```js
// node repro.mjs /absolute/path/to/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent
import { mkdtempSync, writeFileSync, symlinkSync, rmSync } from "node:fs";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join, resolve } from "node:path";
import { pathToFileURL, fileURLToPath } from "node:url";
import { spawnSync } from "node:child_process";
const packageDir = resolve(process.argv[2]);
const { FileAuthStorageBackend } = await import(pathToFileURL(join(packageDir, "dist/core/auth-storage.js")));
if (process.argv[3] === "--child") {
  try {
    await new FileAuthStorageBackend(process.argv[4]).withLockAsync(async () => {
      console.log("ENTERED"); return { result: undefined };
    }, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(500) });
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.name !== "TimeoutError" && e.name !== "AbortError") throw e;
    console.log("BLOCKED");
  }
} else {
  const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "pi-auth-lock-repro-"));
  const canonical = join(dir, "auth.json"), alias = join(dir, "alias.json");
  writeFileSync(canonical, "{}"); symlinkSync(canonical, alias);
  try {
    for (const outerPath of [canonical, alias]) {
      await new FileAuthStorageBackend(outerPath).withLockAsync(async () => {
        const child = spawnSync(process.execPath, [fileURLToPath(import.meta.url), packageDir, "--child", canonical], { encoding: "utf8", timeout: 5000 });
        if (child.status !== 0) throw new Error(child.stderr || String(child.error));
        console.log(`${outerPath === canonical ? "same path" : "symlink alias"}: ${child.stdout.trim()}`);
        return { result: undefined };
      });
    }
  } finally { rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true }); }
}
```

Actual output on 1.3.0:
```text
same path: BLOCKED
symlink alias: ENTERED
```
Expected: `BLOCKED` in both cases. The first line is the positive control.

## Proposed fix
After making sure auth.json exists, resolve it with `fs.realpath`, and use that canonical path for locking, reading and writing, in both `withLock` and `withLockAsync`. Or drop `realpath: false` from both proper-lockfile calls, since the file already exists before acquisition. Take care with a store that doesn't exist yet and with symlinked parent directories.

A regression test: two processes with different aliases of one temp auth file must never overlap in their modify callbacks; cover sync and async acquisition, plus the canonical-path control.

Possibly related, but a different failure mode: #11094 (stale lock directories).

## Summary

A `/reload` of unchanged code can change the system prompt's tool list order, so the next Anthropic request misses the prompt cache. Nothing before the tool definitions is reused, and Anthropic drops the earlier thinking blocks with `prefix_binding_mismatch`. The `tools` array is not affected: it follows the transcript's declared order (`getCurrentTools`), while `<tools>` and the guideline lines follow the active-tool order.

## Cause

`_applyToolLoadout` (`packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts:1671-1674` at b882d08) orders the loadout as "already active, then newly requested". On reload, `reload()` keeps the previous loadout as pending names, but only tools that are already registered are activated. A tool that an extension registers late keeps its place in the transcript, but it is appended to the active order. Example: a tool registered in `session_start` after an `await`. A tool registered at load time stays where it was, so the late tool now comes after it. Any `setActiveTools()` call that deactivates a tool also clears the pending names (`setActiveToolsByName`). `_rebuildSystemPrompt` then renders `<tools>` and the guidelines in that new order. The sections differ from the transcript, so a new system message is recorded and the prompt prefix changes. The same applies on resume (`_restoreToolsFromTranscript`).

Seen with pi 1.5.2 in the TUI: `codemode` is registered at load and activated by the MCP extension at startup. An extension's `ask_user` tools are registered in `session_start` after an await. Before the reload the list is `ask_user, ask_user_resolve, ask_user_adopt, codemode`; after it, `codemode, ask_user, ...`.

## Repro (SDK)

1. Write a transcript whose system message declares `[..., "late_tool", "early_tool"]`.
2. Extension A registers `early_tool` at load. Extension B registers `late_tool` in `session_start` after `await setTimeout(10)`. Both tools have a `promptSnippet`.
3. Run `createAgentSession`, then `bindExtensions(...)`. `session.systemPrompt` lists `early_tool` before `late_tool`, which does not match the declared order. A `reload()` gives the same order.

## Proposed fix

Keep tools the transcript already declares in their declared order. Tools it does not declare yet follow, in their current order:

```diff
 	private _applyToolLoadout(toolNames: string[]): AgentTool[] {
 		const requested = new Set(toolNames);
 		const kept = this.getActiveToolNames().filter((name) => requested.has(name));
-		const tools = [...new Set([...kept, ...requested])].flatMap((name) => {
+		// Tools the transcript already declares keep their declared order, so a reload or resume that
+		// registers them in another order renders the same prompt sections.
+		const declared = getCurrentTools(this.agent.state.messages).map((tool) => tool.name);
+		const rank = (name: string) => {
+			const index = declared.indexOf(name);
+			return index < 0 ? declared.length : index;
+		};
+		const ordered = [...new Set([...kept, ...requested])]
+			.map((name, index) => [name, index] as const)
+			.sort((a, b) => rank(a[0]) - rank(b[0]) || a[1] - b[1])
+			.map(([name]) => name);
+		const tools = ordered.flatMap((name) => {
```

This keeps #11495's guarantee: reordering the same names still does not change the prompt.

## Measured (pi 1.5.2, TUI, about 118k context, reload of identical code)

| | system field | first request after the reload: cacheRead / cacheWrite |
|---|---|---|
| unpatched | `<tools>` and guidelines reordered | 35,057 / 83,089 (30%) |
| patched | byte-identical | 116,383 / 1,183 (99%) |

